The uid() function uses Date.now() and an incrementing counter, but idCounter is a module-level variable that persists across provider instances. If the provider is unmounted and remounted, idCounter keeps incrementing but Date.now() might not have changed much, potentially creating ID collisions. Consider resetting idCounter or using a more robust ID generation approach like crypto.randomUUID().
```suggestion
function uid() {
  if (typeof crypto !== "undefined" && typeof crypto.randomUUID === "function") {
    return `pw_${crypto.randomUUID()}`
  }
  // Fallback for environments without crypto.randomUUID
  return `pw_${Date.now().toString(36)}_${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2)}`
```

The replace operation on line 32 will only replace the first occurrence of "</head>". If the HTML contains multiple </head> tags (malformed HTML), the polyfill stub will be inserted in the wrong location. Use a more robust approach like parsing the HTML with DOMParser or use a global regex replace, or better yet, insert the script before the first </head> closing tag specifically.
```suggestion
      const headCloseTag = "</head>"
      const headCloseIndex = html.indexOf(headCloseTag)
      if (headCloseIndex !== -1) {
        html =
          html.slice(0, headCloseIndex) +
          `${stub}\n${headCloseTag}` +
          html.slice(headCloseIndex + headCloseTag.length)
      } else {
        html = `${stub}\n` + html
      }
```

The postMessage call uses a wildcard "*" as targetOrigin, which allows any origin to receive messages. This is a security risk as it exposes potentially sensitive data (user prompts, AI responses) to malicious iframes. Since the sandbox is loaded via srcdoc, you should use a specific origin or at minimum verify the source before posting. Consider using window.location.origin or implementing origin validation.
```suggestion
  iframe.contentWindow?.postMessage(msg, window.location.origin)
}

